   "id": "d3a23f83-13b6-477a-9b5e-4084996d0906",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Discussion 2: The Electric Double Layer\n",
    "\n",
    "Despite its seeming simplicity, a definitive understanding of the exact structure of the electric double layer that forms at an electrode surface has proven elusive. Scientific studies on this layer continue to this day, with much being published in top-level academic journals. \n",
    "\n",
    "Several articles are listed below to provide you a sample of current research in the field. Each of these articles is focused on some aspect of the electric double layer. \n",
    "- **Read through the articles linked below before class.** \n",
    "- Highlight parts of the articles that you found informative and/or confusing. \n",
    "- Come to class ready to raise questions and/or discuss pertinent points of the articles.\n",
    "- You should not feel like you fully understand each article you read -- even many experts in the field may struggle to fully understand what is written\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ae5a0884-6cc0-4fc9-8a12-ebd3f284a9d2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Correlated surface-charging behaviors of two electrodes in an electrochemical cell\n",
    "\n",
    "[2023, Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U. S. A., 120, e2307307120](https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.2307307120)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6f61f09a-afad-4ef2-b10a-869aa8a80742",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Absence of CO$_2$ electroreduction on copper, gold and silver electrodes without metal cations in solution\n",
